<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Probability density functions</title>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that you perform an experiment with outcomes <m>0\leq x\leq
  10</m> and that your data is described by the probability density
  function <m>p(x) = \frac 1{50}x</m>.</p>
      <p>Find the median of the data and the average <m>\bar{x}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Two fast food restaurants collect data about how long customers
  wait before their order is taken.  One restaurant finds that the
  waiting time is described by the probability density function
  <md>
  p_1(t) = \frac 12e^{-t/2}
  </md>
  where <m>0 \leq t &lt; \infty</m> is measured in minutes. The second
  restaurant finds the probability density function
  <md>
  p_2(t) = \frac 14e^{-t/4}.
  </md></p>
        <p>Verify that <m>p_1(t)</m> is a probability density function;  that
  is, check that <m>\int_0^\infty p(t)~dt = 1</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>For the first restaurant, what fraction of patrons waits
  less than one minute?  What fraction waits between 1 and 2 minutes?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sketch the graphs of the two functions <m>p_1(t)</m>
  and <m>p_2(t)</m> on the same plot.  Based only on these graphs, which
  restaurant offers generally faster service?  Explain your response.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now find the average waiting time for the two restaurants.
  Does your result confirm what you saw in the graphs?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>C_1(s)</m> is the cumulative distribution function
  for the first restaurant.  Explain what this function measures.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the cumulative distribution function <m>C_1(s)</m> and use it to
  find the median waiting time of the first restaurant.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Is this median lower or higher than the average waiting time?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How do the medians of the two restaurants compare?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose there is a company that makes phones, and they study how
  long it takes for a phone to fail.  A typical probability
  distribution in this case is a ``Gamma distribution,'' such as
  <md>p(x) = \frac{1}{16}x^2e^{-\frac12 x}.</md></p>
        <p>Graph this function and determine the most likely time for a phone
  to fail.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the chance that a phone lasts longer than 4 years?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the average time it takes a phone to fail?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>At what point will 50% of phones have failed?  What's another word
  for this time?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
